My features were not well divided into classes and there was a lot of overlap. Even with refactoring and code improvements.
       
  ---                                                                         
  1. Extract the theme/color scheme into its own module
                                                       
  Right now the dark theme is a hardcoded COLORS dict inside EditorWidget, and StatusBarWidget in main_window.py has more      
  hardcoded hex values scattered inline in setStyleSheet calls. These are the same design decision — the visual theme — spread across two unrelated classes.
                                                                              
  A
  single Theme module with a well-defined interface would mean that if you ever change the palette, you change
  one file instead of hunting across the codebase. Currently, neither EditorWidget nor StatusBarWidget should know 
  what the colors are — they should just ask the theme.

  ---
  2. Separate the find/replace search engine from the editor widget
                                                                                
  EditorWidget currently does two distinct jobs: it renders text and manages the find/replace search state. The regex algorithm, 
  the match list, and navigation index are all hidden inside a display widget. 

  Parnas's point is that the search algorithm is a design decision — one that I've 
  already changed once. A SearchEngine module that owns the match state and exposes a 
  clean interface would hide that decision. EditorWidget would only need to call it and apply the resulting
  selections — it wouldn't need to know how the matches were found. This also takes the search logic independently testable without instantiating a Qt
  widget.
